  "notes": "Long two-storey bar on a ridge above the valley, the view side (balcony, lawn) taken as south. From the long lawn elevation (fieldstone ground floor set back behind concrete columns, a continuous cantilevered floor slab with beam ends and a steel rail, the upper floor glazed in black frames between concrete piers, a thin cantilevered roof with beam stubs along its edge, the board-formed concrete chimney), the back elevation (the upper storey a closed concrete band with large punched windows and a strip of low windows, fieldstone below), the west-end view (roof and floor cantilevered over an open corner terrace, a tall concrete blade rising past the roof along the north side) and the cactus-garden and path views (a paved path and gravel along the stone base, a concrete blade standing off the east end). This pass, so the layered edge reads from above: the fieldstone is 'rubble', darker than the concrete, so the base shows as its own layer under the slab; the upper glazing gets its black frames (mullions every 1.6 m, head and sill), so the glass reads as a dark band between the two slabs; the south ground-floor windows are larger and dark-headed as in the lawn view; the roof is thinner (0.4 m) and the north band no longer stands up as a parapet along its length (the photographs show it stopping under the roof) \u2014 only the west-end blade rises past the roof, longer and taller as in the west view; the balcony rail is 'mesh' (open, dark bars) rather than a solid steel sheet, and returns round the west end, so from above the edge reads roof / dark rail / slab / stone; the balcony is 1.8 m deep, the roof edge flush with the slab edge as in the lawn view; timber floors show through the glass; the lawn bank has its two rows of field stones; a paved path runs along the north side; agaves and prickly pear join the cacti. Still guessed: depth (~10 m) and the exact positions of ground-floor openings; the stepping of the north band's volumes seen in the west view is simplified to one blade; the red succulents on the bank are left out."
